(*
expect:
set 0
reads nil
still ringing
finished
*)
(* tuvm#100 — a clock carrying a factory-default date is NOT a clock, even though
   every field in it is well-formed.

   THIS IS THE TEST FOR A FAILURE THAT HAPPENED. On the first hardware run of this
   seam (C6 + DS3231, 2026-07-31) the module answered a clean I2C read with
   2000-01-01 00:00:00 — it had never been set, or its CR2032 had gone flat with
   mains off. Zephyr's rtc_get_time documents -ENODATA for exactly this
   (include/zephyr/drivers/rtc.h:212) and the DS3231 driver NEVER RETURNS IT: it
   reads seven registers and converts them (drivers/rtc/rtc_ds3231.c:339), never
   consulting the part's own oscillator-stop flag. So the read succeeds, rc is 0,
   and the answer is 00:00:00.

   00:00:00 is seconds-of-day 0, which is midnight, which is when a ship's bell
   strikes EIGHT. Trusting rc alone would make a flat backup cell ring the loudest
   thing the product does, at the moment it is least expected, and go on doing it.
   Hence the guard, and hence this test: the seam must answer nil for an
   implausible year rather than hand a node a number that is technically valid.

   2000 is not an arbitrary bad value — it is the DS3231's own power-on year, so
   this test states the exact reading the hardware produces. The guard is a year
   comparison rather than a check of the oscillator-stop flag only because that
   flag is unreachable from here: the driver's accessor is static and compiled only
   under CONFIG_RTC_ALARM. Making get_time answer -ENODATA when OSF is set is the
   real fix and it is upstream's.

   The last two lines are 02's claim, restated for this path: an untrustworthy
   clock is an ORDINARY answer a node branches on, not a raise. A bell whose clock
   cannot be trusted must fall back to free-running, and it must never strike a
   time it does not know. *)
class WallClockUnsetYear packageUsing: manifest = () (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | rc secs |
    (* Exactly what a factory-fresh DS3231 reports: 2000-01-01, midnight. *)
    rc:: platform wallClockSetDate: 20000101 time: 0.
    ('set ' , rc printString) out.

    secs:: platform wallClock.
    ('reads ' , secs printString) out.

    (* No handler in sight: an untrustworthy clock is an answer, not a raise. *)
    'still ringing' out.
    'finished' out.
  )
) : (
)
